+
+# Decision: Concurrent Visit Edit Reconciliation
+
+## Context
+
+The HLD assigns the concurrency mechanism to the record-store node: when two field workers independently correct the same visit while their tablets are offline, the record store decides how those submissions become a single current record. About 6,000 plot visits arrive per season across 12 shared tablets and 24 seasonal workers; two coordinators resolve questions before a reviewed CSV goes to a regional science partner each Friday. Updates can arrive days after the state they were based on was fetched, so arrival order is not evidence that one worker's observations are more authoritative.
+
+Every original submission must be preserved — the HLD tenets *Do not silently discard a submitted edit* and *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable* hold regardless of the mechanism. Both candidate mechanisms satisfy them; they differ in how much of the reconciliation is automatic and what the current record's provenance looks like. The choice determines submission metadata, the shape of coordinator review work, and which record states can enter an export, so it sizes the tablet-sync and review-export designs that follow.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+Gates: none. Both mechanisms clear the inherited invariants (preserve original submissions; operable by two coordinators without a dedicated systems administrator); those are background, not differentiators.
+
+- **Scientific safety — the current record must not silently combine edits whose scientific relationship the mechanism cannot detect** (**major**). Importance derives from the tenet *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*, and from the concern that a computed value blending two workers' intent on related fields (a revised species identification together with its cover estimate) cannot be traced to one coherent observation.
+- **Review workload for two coordinators** (**major**). Importance derives from the tenet *Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator*. Measured as the number of visits per season that require a human resolution.
+- **Export readiness by the Friday deadline** (**moderate**). The fraction of visits that can enter the reviewed CSV without a pending manual resolution.
+- **Implementation and maintenance burden** (**moderate**). The logic the team must author, test, and keep correct as the visit schema evolves. Importance derives from the tenet *Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator*.
+- **Submission size and offline footprint** (**minor**). What each edit must carry from a tablet that may be offline for 48 hours.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+**Description.** Each edit names the revision it was based on and is stored as a complete, intact revision of the visit. Accepted revisions that share a base and have no resolution are competing revisions; a coordinator resolves them by choosing the current state. Nothing is merged at the field level: the current record is always one worker's submitted visit, or a coordinator's explicit resolution. The mechanism needs little merge logic — essentially revision lineage, competing-revision detection, and a resolution operation — and keeps every candidate value attributable to a single submission.
+
+**Demonstration.** Two workers fetch visit V at revision R0. One corrects an observer name (R1); the other adds a missing species and revises a cover estimate (R2). Both are retained intact as competing revisions over R0 and both appear in the review queue; a coordinator selects one. The unrelated observer-name fix still costs a review, because the store does not combine R1 and R2.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Scientific safety: **strong**. No automatic combination occurs, so the store can never silently blend related edits; every current value traces to one intact submission or an explicit resolution.
+- Review workload: **weak**. Every concurrent edit, including edits to unrelated fields, creates review work — a larger queue at 6,000 visits/season.
+- Export readiness: **weak**. Visits with competing revisions stay out of the CSV until resolved, so fewer visits are export-ready at any moment.
+- Implementation and maintenance burden: **strong**. Little merge logic; the visit schema can grow without new per-field combination rules.
+- Submission size / offline footprint: **partial**. Edits carry a complete visit payload plus a base revision ID rather than a compact delta.
+
+**Summary.** Keeps whole-visit intent intact and inspectable at the cost of a larger, fully manual review queue.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+**Description.** Each edit supplies its base state and a set of structured field changes. The server three-way-merges against the base: edits to disjoint fields combine automatically, and only overlapping changes go to review. Scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries each get maintained combination rules, and the current record carries field-level provenance. This is a well-worn technique that keeps review volume proportional to genuine conflicts rather than to every concurrent edit.
+
+**Demonstration.** With the same R0, R1 (observer name) and R2 (missing species, cover estimate) touch disjoint fields, so the server produces a combined current record automatically and the visit is export-ready with no coordinator action. Only a true overlap — both workers editing the same species' cover — would enter review.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Scientific safety: **weak**. The rules operate on field names, not on scientific relationships; they will combine a revised species identification with a separately edited cover estimate as "disjoint" even when the two are related, producing a value no worker submitted and that is hard to attribute.
+- Review workload: **strong**. Only overlapping edits create review work — a substantially smaller queue, which matters with two coordinators and 6,000 visits/season.
+- Export readiness: **strong**. Disjoint edits resolve automatically, so more visits are export-ready before Friday.
+- Implementation and maintenance burden: **weak**. Requires authored, tested, and versioned rules for scalars, lists, and nested species entries, plus field-level provenance and a review view for overlaps; the rules need upkeep as the visit schema changes.
+- Submission size / offline footprint: **partial**. Edits carry a base snapshot and structured changes; compact for small edits, but the tablet must retain base snapshots to compute deltas.
+
+**Summary.** Reduces the review queue and gets more visits to export, but automates exactly the combinations the team is unwilling to make without noticing scientific relationships.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Whole-visit revisions.** The two major criteria decide it: scientific safety (**major**) is strong for whole-visit revisions and weak for field-aware merge, and the weak review-workload fit (**major**) is the accepted cost — a larger, fully manual review queue is preferable to maintained merge rules that can combine scientifically related edits without recognizing the relationship. Whole-visit revisions are also strong on implementation and maintenance burden (**moderate**), which offsets part of that cost; weaker export readiness (**moderate**) is accepted by holding unresolved visits out of the CSV.
+
+Field-aware three-way merge stays credible: it genuinely cuts coordinator review volume, gets more visits to the Friday export, and rests on a well-worn technique. Those gains do not outweigh the risk of silently blending related edits and the ongoing cost of per-field combination rules, so the team takes the larger queue.
+
+The decision turns on the tenets *Do not silently discard a submitted edit* and *Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable*: a current record is always one intact submitted visit or an explicit, attributable coordinator resolution, so provenance stays legible. It is taken against the pull of *Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate without a dedicated systems administrator* only in the narrow sense that review volume rises; the queue remains a manual, coordinator-operable workflow with no merge machinery to maintain.
+
+It forecloses automatic field combination and field-level provenance: the record store never computes a blended current value. It obligates downstream work:
+
+- **Tablet-sync** retains the base revision ID of the visit state a worker displayed, keeps local drafts across restarts and multi-day disconnection, submits complete visit revisions, and shows pending competing revisions with an explanation of server outcomes.
+- **Review-export** provides a revision comparison screen over competing whole revisions, records a coordinator's resolution, and defines export eligibility so visits with unresolved competing revisions do not enter the reviewed CSV. Its Friday export produces the partner's existing columns from the selected whole revision.
+
+These are obligations on sibling nodes, to be specified in their own designs; they are not defined here.
