+A visit's scientific fields include observation date, observer list, species cover estimates, and notes. Edits to these fields are preserved as a unit within each revision. Some edits are unrelated (correcting an observer name, adding a missing species); others are related (revising a species identification together with its cover estimate). Because the server cannot infer such relationships from field names, it does not attempt field-level combination.
 
 ## Submission and Retrieval
 
-Authenticate the submitting worker and validate field types and plot references before accepting a submission. Preserve the original payload separately from any later resolved or combined representation. A retry with the same key and identical payload returns the original acceptance result; reuse with a different payload is rejected.
+Authenticate the submitting worker and validate field types and plot references before accepting a submission. Each submission names the revision ID of the visit state it was based on and carries the complete field payload. Preserve the original submission, its author, and its receipt time separately from any resolved current state. A retry with the same idempotency key and identical payload returns the original acceptance result; reuse with a different payload is rejected.
 
-Retrieval must distinguish a usable current state from unresolved review work. Exactly how those states are represented remains part of the open choice.
+Current-state retrieval distinguishes a visit with a single agreed current revision from one with unresolved competing revisions. Submission-history retrieval returns every accepted original revision, including those not selected by a resolution.
 
-## Open Design Choice: Concurrent Visit Edits
+## Whole-Visit Revisions
 
-OPEN: select the concurrency policy before completing this node's public contract.
+Each accepted edit becomes a new, complete revision identified by a server-assigned revision ID and linked to the base revision it names. Two or more revisions that name the same base are competing revisions for that visit until a resolution is recorded; none of their fields are merged automatically. A coordinator resolves the set by establishing the visit's current revision (the exact resolution semantics remain an open question below).
 
-Two approaches remain credible:
+A resolution records the coordinator's identity, the contributing revision IDs, and the resulting current revision. Losing revisions are retained in submission history; no submitted visit is discarded.
 
-- Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync would retain base revision IDs and display pending competing revisions. Review-export would need a revision comparison workflow and exclude unresolved visits from its export set.
-- Field-aware three-way merge: each edit supplies its base state and changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically; overlapping changes require review. This reduces review volume and gets more visits ready for export, but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Automatically combined fields can also cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. Tablet-sync would retain base snapshots and send structured changes. Review-export would need field-level provenance and a review view for overlapping changes.
+## Downstream Contract Work
 
-The choice trades review workload and submission size against implementation burden and preservation of whole-visit intent. Neither approach may discard original submissions. Field-aware merging is not ruled out by a requirement that every combined record receive manual approval.
+The selected mechanism sets the contract that tablet-sync and review-export are designed against. These are obligations on those sibling nodes, recorded here as a cascade; their requirements belong in those nodes' own spec files and are not defined in this document.
 
-## Downstream Contract Work
+- Tablet-sync retains the base revision ID of the visit state a worker displayed, keeps local drafts across restarts and multi-day disconnection, submits complete visit revisions, and shows pending competing revisions with an explanation of server outcomes.
+- Review-export provides a revision comparison screen over competing whole revisions, records a coordinator's resolution, and defines export eligibility so visits with unresolved competing revisions do not enter the reviewed CSV. Its Friday export produces the partner's existing columns from the selected whole revision.
+
+These obligations are noted for those nodes' design work; neither node is designed yet.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Concurrent-edit reconciliation | Whole-visit revisions: retain each submitted visit intact and route competing complete revisions to a coordinator | Field-aware three-way merge (auto-combine disjoint field edits, review only overlaps) | A larger, fully manual review queue is preferred to maintained merge rules that could combine scientifically related edits — e.g. a revised species identification together with its cover estimate — without recognizing the relationship. Field-aware merge remains credible because it sharply reduces coordinator review volume and gets more visits export-ready before Friday, and three-way merge is a well-worn technique; those benefits do not outweigh the risk of silently blending related edits and the cost of maintaining per-field combination rules. Whole-visit revisions keep each submission intact as a unit and require no field-combination logic. Full trade-off: decisions/concurrent-edit-reconciliation.md. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Deferred
+1. Resolution semantics: does a coordinator select one of the competing revisions as the current state, or author a fresh corrected revision? May a coordinator correct values, or only select?
+2. Current-state response while a visit has unresolved competing revisions: return the last agreed (base) revision flagged unresolved, return no current state, or designate a provisional one?
+3. Revision lineage past the common case: how is an edit based on an already-superseded revision treated (new competing branch, rejected, or reopen), does a resolution become the base for later edits, and what does a late revision naming a pre-resolution base do after that set was resolved?
+4. Idempotency key scope (per visit, per worker, or global) and whether a resolution carries its own idempotency key.
+5. API error shape for an edit that names a nonexistent or foreign base revision.
+
+## References
 
-After selection, define the submission envelope, current-state response, unresolved-work representation, and resolution operation here. Tablet-sync will size its local storage and retry state machine around that envelope. Review-export will size its coordinator interface and export eligibility rules around the unresolved-work representation. Both must link back to this node rather than invent their own concurrency semantics.
